<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Confucius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">551–<a href="/years/479-bce">479 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Kongzi, Kong Qiu, Kong Fuzi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Confucius (Kong Qiu, Kongzi) was a teacher and minor official of the State of Lu whose sayings, collected by his disciples in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a>, became the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>. He taught that good government rests on ritual propriety, benevolence and the moral example of the ruler, and travelled among the states of Wey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Chen and Cai seeking office. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> his teaching formed the core of state orthodoxy and education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucius.jpg" alt="Confucius" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Chinese painter of the Western Han period · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius,_fresco_from_a_Western_Han_tomb_of_Dongping_County,_Shandong_province,_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
